f 17592186045417] [17592186413340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413340 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413340 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413340 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413340 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd5de39b-4f47-50b7-a647-9365f8fad97f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413341 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413341 #edb/ref 1069 "q-88693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413341 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413341 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413341 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413341 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413342 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413342 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334371" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413342 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413342 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e16dfe4-483e-56b2-ae34-2f1386783053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413342 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413343 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413343 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413343 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413343 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413343 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413344 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413344 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1148328-1d0d-53fb-826c-7f5db78f1f65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413344 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\sqrt{4-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413345 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413345 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0277a93a-ee4